    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Syst??????me de cote ??????nerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="6" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.6b18345</English>
                <French>v15.6b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b18345</English>
                <French>v1.16b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls>

Your house has a manufactured home on the upper floor that sits on top of a stick-built main floor. The main floor is 2x6@16 construction with R20 batt insulation, no exterior wall sheathing and a wood panel exterior. The upper floor is 2x6@24 construction with R20 batt construction, no exterior sheathing and a wood panel exterior. From the picture you sent me about the main floor wall repair, it looks like the vapour barrier is correctly positioned on the interior surface of the 2x6 studs. The exterior walls of your house and the left addition appear to be 2x4 construction with no more than R12 insulation. When I look at the rear lower corner of the upper floor (where the birdhouse was) it looks like there is a vapour barrier on the exterior of the 2x6 studs, which would be on the wrong side - this should be investigated further.



We discussed the potential for you to add more insulation to the exterior of both the lower &amp; upper floor walls, in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding R20 to the exterior of your walls.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10 (adding 2" of rigid foam board insulation beneath new siding), you could potentially save $ 960 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



Please note that completing a DHERP wall insulation upgrade could qualify for you for some other rebates that you would otherwise not receive:

    - Furnace upgrade rebate is doubled from $1,500 to $3,000

    - An estimated air sealing rebate of $500

    - Window rebates of $250/eligible window (up to 12 windows max)

    - An estimated $2,000 Bonus rebate for reducing your overall energy use by 30% - 39% (this includes the furnace upgrade savings).



In your case, you really need to look at the cost of the wall insulation upgrade vs. the estimated benefits. You would obtain the cost by obtaining quotes. The estimated benefits include:

    - annual energy savings of about $960

    - wall insulation rebate of $3,000

    - other incremental rebates of $4,000 ($1,500 extra for furnace, $500 air sealing &amp; $2,000 bonus)



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 284 litre electric hot water tank. With 6 occupants, you volume of hot water use will be quite high (showers, laundry, dishes, etc.) and the cost of electricity in Yellowknife is quite high. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 1,031 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an oil furnace (83% efficiency) that is the primary heating system for both the main and 2nd floor. With the thermostat that controls the furnace being upstairs, it is natural for the upper floor to be warmer than the lower floor. For the spare bedroom in the front section of the main floor, there is an Empire wall furnace installed for supplemental heat. Your house has a wood burning fireplace on the upper floor that you generally do not use ....... only there as a backup heat source. For the primary furnace, we talked about closing down some of the heat vents on the upper floor (to force more heat supply to the main floor) and also trying to establish the main "return air" to the furnace from the floor level of the main floor.



The current furnace vents up a 6" chimney and there is also a 5" combustion air supply pipe to the furnace room. There is also a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace, so some outside fresh air is drawn in and circulated throughout the house whenever the furnace is operating. These fresh air supply pipes usually have a damper installed so you can manually open or partially/fully close the air supply depending on how much fresh air the house needs in winter (ie: if lots of humidity or frost on windows in winter, increase the amount of fresh air supply &amp; perhaps operate the range hood or a bathroom fan to eliminate excess humidity). From the blower door air leakage test I conducted, your house appears to be quite air tight and may require some level of fresh air to the furnace ductwork during the heating season ......... especially with up to 6 occupants in the house. 



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace will eliminate the current 6" chimney and the 5" combustion air supply pipe, however the 5" fresh air supply pipe should be maintained - perhaps have your heating contractor install a damper so you can adjust the volume of fresh air entering your house.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 2,073 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 50.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and the two on the upper floor are older 13 litre model, and you should consider replacing them with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has various components that contribute to ventilation:

        - the kitchen range hood fan vents to the outside

        - there are 3 x bathroom fans that all vent to the outside

        - there are 2 x clothes dryers that exhaust to the exterior

        - there is a 5" fresh air (make-up air) supply pipe connected to the return plenum of the furnace



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seals well, use little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. You currently have one of these in your master bathroom.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, 3 x bathroom fans and both dryers), indicated a pressure drop of 14.4 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace does atmospherically vent up a chimney, and could be susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into your house. The wood fireplace does not have sealed doors and it also susceptible to back-drafting. The Empire Wall Furnace is a "direct vent" unit that is less susceptible to back-drafting.



If you upgrade the furnace to sealed combustion unit (as outlined elsewhere in this report), that type of furnace is not susceptible to back-drafting .......... and the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipe for the existing oil furnace can be eliminated.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre delivered

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportati</AdditionalComments>
